WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.836
This is the SD-WAN deep
dive for Book 09 of the

3
00:00:03.836 --> 00:00:07.850
Federal Modernization Workbook,
Network and Transport.

4
00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:11.000
It's for the engineers and
architects who'll design,

5
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.875
secure and evidence a branch fabric, and
it stays on SD-WAN the whole way through.

6
00:00:15.875 --> 00:00:21.312
Part 1 is transport and the branch
edge: the physics SD-WAN works against,

7
00:00:21.312 --> 00:00:26.012
then what TIC 3.0 and
Microsoft 365 ask of a branch.

8
00:00:26.012 --> 00:00:30.337
Part 2 is governing and transforming:
protecting the fabric itself,

9
00:00:30.337 --> 00:00:35.049
and a worked field-office estate with
real sizing and an honest cost model.

10
00:00:35.049 --> 00:00:38.720
Let's start with what SD-WAN
actually is, and isn't.

11
00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:45.020
Here's the frame for
everything that follows.

12
00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:48.470
People say SD-WAN when they
mean three different things.

13
00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:53.582
SD-WAN decides which path traffic takes
and measures how each path is doing.

14
00:00:53.582 --> 00:00:58.270
SASE, above it, decides whether a
flow is allowed and inspects it.

15
00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:01.144
And the governed path,
which is still a proposal,

16
00:01:01.144 --> 00:01:03.870
asks whether the path
itself was authorized.

17
00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:06.620
The pipes underneath close almost nothing.

18
00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:10.731
Then there's the question most sources
skip: who protects the governor,

19
00:01:10.731 --> 00:01:13.495
now that one controller
can steer every branch?

20
00:01:13.495 --> 00:01:15.545
So the honest claim is narrow.

21
00:01:15.545 --> 00:01:18.000
Let's see how everything fits in order.

22
00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:25.065
The book's practical rule
is an order of operations,

23
00:01:25.065 --> 00:01:27.275
and each module hangs off one step.

24
00:01:27.275 --> 00:01:28.987
Secure the governor first.

25
00:01:28.987 --> 00:01:30.987
Buy the pipe on measured physics.

26
00:01:30.987 --> 00:01:34.512
Have the governor live and
logging before production traffic.

27
00:01:34.512 --> 00:01:37.175
Decide the boundary change before cutover.

28
00:01:37.175 --> 00:01:40.963
Handle Microsoft 365
through its own exception.

29
00:01:40.963 --> 00:01:44.672
And register the evidence,
including CISA's telemetry,

30
00:01:44.672 --> 00:01:46.850
before any old circuit goes away.

31
00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:50.483
The book is clear that this
is an engineering dependency,

32
00:01:50.483 --> 00:01:53.962
not a mandate, which is exactly
why it holds up in review.

33
00:01:53.962 --> 00:01:57.900
Module 1 starts with the physics
the pipe is bought against.

34
00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:03.975
Module 1 is transport physics.

35
00:02:03.975 --> 00:02:08.603
SD-WAN claims tend to fail in one of
two ways: promising the impossible,

36
00:02:08.603 --> 00:02:12.825
or underselling what's real, which
is mostly in the latency tail.

37
00:02:12.825 --> 00:02:17.408
We'll separate the floor nothing moves
from the gap where SD-WAN does its work,

38
00:02:17.408 --> 00:02:22.490
and finish with what a pipe closes on its
own, which turns out to be just one thing.

39
00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.352
Every round trip has two parts.

40
00:02:28.352 --> 00:02:31.927
One is propagation, light in
fiber covering the distance,

41
00:02:31.927 --> 00:02:33.828
and nothing you buy changes it.

42
00:02:33.828 --> 00:02:37.037
The other is everything
else: queueing, jitter,

43
00:02:37.037 --> 00:02:40.078
loss, and routes longer
than a straight line.

44
00:02:40.078 --> 00:02:44.165
That second part is the only thing
any SD-WAN feature can touch.

45
00:02:44.165 --> 00:02:46.502
So the test for a proposal is simple.

46
00:02:46.502 --> 00:02:49.440
Claim to beat the floor,
and it hasn't been checked.

47
00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:53.277
Claim to shrink the gap, and it
might be true, so measure it.

48
00:02:53.277 --> 00:02:56.677
The book also asks you to
label every number you use.

49
00:02:56.677 --> 00:02:58.650
Now, how big is that gap?

50
00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.875
This is how much of the gap path
selection can actually win back.

51
00:03:05.875 --> 00:03:10.300
Measured internet paths run at roughly
twice the straight-fiber floor.

52
00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:14.009
A premium provider backbone,
on the book's worked example

53
00:03:14.009 --> 00:03:17.875
from London to Virginia, runs
at about one point three times.

54
00:03:17.875 --> 00:03:20.623
That ratio, not a vendor's
claim, is the ceiling:

55
00:03:20.623 --> 00:03:25.006
steering lean traffic onto a better
underlay recovers the difference

56
00:03:25.006 --> 00:03:29.463
between an internet path and a backbone
path, and nothing below the floor.

57
00:03:29.463 --> 00:03:32.963
And remember, a faster circuit
doesn't straighten the route.

58
00:03:32.963 --> 00:03:34.913
Only where the traffic goes does.

59
00:03:34.913 --> 00:03:37.070
So which traffic should go where?

60
00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:45.458
SD-WAN's core move is putting each class
of traffic on the path built for it.

61
00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:48.100
Lean traffic is the small, urgent stuff:

62
00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:53.796
database heartbeats and commit acknowledgements,
and at a branch, voice and sign-in.

63
00:03:53.796 --> 00:03:57.120
Bulk is backups, re-syncs and patch waves.

64
00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.595
The insight is that this mirrors a
choice the data layer already makes,

65
00:04:01.595 --> 00:04:04.295
between strong and eventual consistency.

66
00:04:04.295 --> 00:04:08.483
When the two agree, you get the best
synchronization physics allows.

67
00:04:08.483 --> 00:04:13.207
And there's a neat clock angle: spend
clock certainty instead of round trips.

68
00:04:13.207 --> 00:04:15.558
Clock sync rides the lean path too.

69
00:04:15.558 --> 00:04:18.680
Next, the part of SD-WAN people underrate.

70
00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:25.267
This is where SD-WAN
quietly earns its keep.

71
00:04:25.267 --> 00:04:30.031
A quorum commit or a voice call is
only as good as its unluckiest packet,

72
00:04:30.031 --> 00:04:32.906
so what matters is the
tail, not the average.

73
00:04:32.906 --> 00:04:36.023
Forward error correction
rebuilds lost packets

74
00:04:36.023 --> 00:04:39.293
without waiting for a retry,
at the cost of bandwidth.

75
00:04:39.293 --> 00:04:44.280
Packet duplication sends critical packets
down two paths, if you have two links.

76
00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.655
Fast failover moves traffic
before TCP even notices trouble.

77
00:04:48.655 --> 00:04:52.617
And class separation stops a backup
from queuing ahead of a call.

78
00:04:52.617 --> 00:04:55.330
A ragged path can end
up close to the floor.

79
00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:57.340
Now, the pipes themselves.

80
00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:07.240
Every underlay, fiber, MPLS, satellite,
cellular or broadband, is a pipe.

81
00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:11.724
Their physics differ enormously,
and the table makes that concrete: a

82
00:05:11.724 --> 00:05:17.152
geostationary satellite path alone blows
the voice budget, while low orbit doesn't.

83
00:05:17.152 --> 00:05:21.841
But their contribution to the security
controls is the same, which is nothing.

84
00:05:21.841 --> 00:05:24.965
MPLS is separation, not encryption.

85
00:05:24.965 --> 00:05:27.534
What a second pipe does
give you is diversity,

86
00:05:27.534 --> 00:05:31.353
and a branch with local internet
plus diverse cellular can show it.

87
00:05:31.353 --> 00:05:35.189
Just remember each new circuit
is another interface to govern.

88
00:05:35.189 --> 00:05:39.765
So diversity closes one family
and quietly enlarges another.

89
00:05:39.765 --> 00:05:41.780
That wraps up Module 1.

90
00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:47.780
Module 2 is the branch edge.

91
00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:51.353
Most agencies with local breakout
already pair it with SASE,

92
00:05:51.353 --> 00:05:53.105
and that's the right direction.

93
00:05:53.105 --> 00:05:55.640
But SASE changes where inspection happens,

94
00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:58.243
not whether the stretch
before it is governed.

95
00:05:58.243 --> 00:06:03.076
We'll cover that gap, what TIC
3.0 asks of the steering policy,

96
00:06:03.076 --> 00:06:06.926
where SASE should sit, how to
pick a pattern for each site,

97
00:06:06.926 --> 00:06:10.720
and the Microsoft 365
decision every branch faces.

98
00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:18.358
Here's why a branch-edge layer
still matters once SASE is in place.

99
00:06:18.358 --> 00:06:20.779
With just an internet
circuit and a router,

100
00:06:20.779 --> 00:06:24.446
nothing at the branch notices a
link going bad short of an outage.

101
00:06:24.446 --> 00:06:28.057
Sign-in and backups share
the same unmanaged queue.

102
00:06:28.057 --> 00:06:29.807
A second circuit sits idle.

103
00:06:29.807 --> 00:06:33.895
And interface counters can't tell you
which application saw which delay.

104
00:06:33.895 --> 00:06:38.219
The governed branch isn't more secure
at the point of presence; it's governed

105
00:06:38.219 --> 00:06:42.682
and measured on the way there, which is
the least instrumented part of the path.

106
00:06:42.682 --> 00:06:46.210
Next, what TIC 3.0 asks of that edge.

107
00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:55.860
TIC 3.0 never requires SD-WAN; it
names it once, as something to govern.

108
00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:00.326
But two sentences in the Branch Office
Use Case bind any steering policy,

109
00:07:00.326 --> 00:07:02.410
and they're worth reading closely.

110
00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:08.335
Every path, across redundant links or
SD-WAN, has to keep the same security parity.

111
00:07:08.335 --> 00:07:13.235
And the points deciding where flows go
mustn't be easy to bypass or manipulate.

112
00:07:13.235 --> 00:07:17.673
Put together, that makes the steering
decision itself a security control.

113
00:07:17.673 --> 00:07:22.081
The planning consequence matters too:
circuits arrive in about a quarter,

114
00:07:22.081 --> 00:07:25.622
but an authorized breakout
architecture takes far longer.

115
00:07:25.622 --> 00:07:27.530
So where does SASE sit?

116
00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:34.342
Three practical points before
you name a SASE service.

117
00:07:34.342 --> 00:07:39.562
First, CISA's model: the security services
handle traffic to the outside world,

118
00:07:39.562 --> 00:07:43.979
SD-WAN routes internal traffic,
and telemetry flows to CISA's CLAW.

119
00:07:43.979 --> 00:07:46.868
Second, the enforcement
point has a location.

120
00:07:46.868 --> 00:07:51.959
A distant or poorly peered point of
presence is just a hairpin with a new name,

121
00:07:51.959 --> 00:07:55.192
so measure branch-to-PoP
latency before cutover.

122
00:07:55.192 --> 00:08:00.410
Third, buying: FedRAMP status is per
offering and covers the cloud service,

123
00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:05.542
not your boxes, and you should confirm
the EIS line items with GSA up front.

124
00:08:05.542 --> 00:08:08.230
Next, choosing a pattern per site.

125
00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:14.705
Every branch ends up in
one of four patterns.

126
00:08:14.705 --> 00:08:16.880
The hairpin is assessed but slow.

127
00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:19.530
Bare breakout is fast, and a regression.

128
00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:23.196
Governed breakout, with an edge
and a nearby security service,

129
00:08:23.196 --> 00:08:24.705
is the reference pattern.

130
00:08:24.705 --> 00:08:28.060
And agent-based SASE suits
small or roaming sites,

131
00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:30.617
if the point of presence really is near.

132
00:08:30.617 --> 00:08:35.092
The guidance is to choose per site
from measured latency and device mix,

133
00:08:35.092 --> 00:08:38.731
never bare breakout, and to
file the boundary change first.

134
00:08:38.731 --> 00:08:43.368
Whatever you pick, DNS still has
to reach the agency's resolver.

135
00:08:43.368 --> 00:08:48.110
And Microsoft 365 needs its
own exception, which is next.

136
00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:54.648
This is the most argued
decision at a branch.

137
00:08:54.648 --> 00:08:57.896
Microsoft says don't inspect
its Optimize traffic;

138
00:08:57.896 --> 00:09:00.372
the boundary says inspect everything.

139
00:09:00.372 --> 00:09:03.251
Both hold up, so it's a
decision someone signs,

140
00:09:03.251 --> 00:09:05.810
not a setting someone quietly changes.

141
00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:10.538
Optimize is small: mail, SharePoint
and OneDrive, and Teams media,

142
00:09:10.538 --> 00:09:14.285
which has no names and must
be steered by IP address.

143
00:09:14.285 --> 00:09:18.309
The program exempts only that,
narrower than Microsoft asks,

144
00:09:18.309 --> 00:09:20.885
and keeps sign-in on the inspected path.

145
00:09:20.885 --> 00:09:24.132
And it names the specific
controls the bypass affects,

146
00:09:24.132 --> 00:09:26.585
rather than writing inspection forgone.

147
00:09:26.585 --> 00:09:29.070
So how is that decision made safely?

148
00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:35.778
The attack an assessor
will try is moving agency

149
00:09:35.778 --> 00:09:38.958
data to some other tenant
over the uninspected path.

150
00:09:38.958 --> 00:09:41.170
Tenant restrictions are the control,

151
00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:45.808
and only the version signaled from the
Windows device protects the data plane

152
00:09:45.808 --> 00:09:50.446
for SharePoint and Exchange, so the
program makes it a precondition to cutover.

153
00:09:50.446 --> 00:09:54.808
Then three documents, each signed by
the person whose question it answers:

154
00:09:54.808 --> 00:09:59.457
the authorizing official, the system
owner and the architecture review board.

155
00:09:59.457 --> 00:10:03.750
And the register's egress addresses
have to be the real post-NAT ones,

156
00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:05.820
verified from the branch itself.

157
00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:07.580
That closes Part 1.

158
00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:14.292
Module 3 turns to the governor itself.

159
00:10:14.292 --> 00:10:19.330
If we credit SD-WAN with closing controls,
we owe the controls that protect it.

160
00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:22.760
We'll walk its attack surface,
make step zero checkable,

161
00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:26.952
look ahead to the governed path, pin
down the evidence it has to emit,

162
00:10:26.952 --> 00:10:31.430
and end with what the fabric owes
the applications that ride on it.

163
00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:38.568
SD-WAN changes the attack
surface in two ways.

164
00:10:38.568 --> 00:10:42.813
A single control plane now has
authority over every branch's routing,

165
00:10:42.813 --> 00:10:47.431
and a fleet of internet-facing edges
replaces a few hardened egress points.

166
00:10:47.431 --> 00:10:49.755
This is real, not hypothetical.

167
00:10:49.755 --> 00:10:54.764
In February 2026 CISA issued an
emergency directive for one vendor's

168
00:10:54.764 --> 00:10:59.255
SD-WAN manager and controller,
after an authentication bypass.

169
00:10:59.255 --> 00:11:04.163
Two standing directives cover the fleet:
one for end-of-support edge devices,

170
00:11:04.163 --> 00:11:07.280
one setting patch windows
as short as three days.

171
00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:11.355
So the working assumption is that edge
flaws land in the shortest window.

172
00:11:11.355 --> 00:11:13.710
Let's make step zero concrete.

173
00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:20.267
Step zero is easy to
say and hard to check,

174
00:11:20.267 --> 00:11:24.860
so this slide pairs each control
area with the evidence to ask for.

175
00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:29.485
Keep management interfaces off the
internet, behind phishing-resistant sign-in.

176
00:11:29.485 --> 00:11:33.297
Onboard edges with certificates,
so a rogue box can't join.

177
00:11:33.297 --> 00:11:38.159
Treat any routing-policy change as a
reviewed change, not a console edit.

178
00:11:38.159 --> 00:11:41.985
Patch on the federal clocks and
track every edge's support date.

179
00:11:41.985 --> 00:11:43.860
Keep logs off the devices.

180
00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:46.895
The best single test is
whether you could inventory,

181
00:11:46.895 --> 00:11:49.647
pull logs and patch the
whole fleet within a day.

182
00:11:49.647 --> 00:11:52.410
Now, where the path itself is heading.

183
00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:58.948
This one is a proposal,
and it's labeled that way.

184
00:11:58.948 --> 00:12:03.894
Put a well-tuned edge on a flat network
and its value is capped: its identity

185
00:12:03.894 --> 00:12:08.922
signals have nowhere to land, and a
hijacked session inherits all the trust.

186
00:12:08.922 --> 00:12:13.498
The proposal treats the path like
a token: leased for one purpose and

187
00:12:13.498 --> 00:12:17.910
expiring with it, so a flow with no
token behind it shows up as drift.

188
00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:20.348
It's careful about lane discipline, too.

189
00:12:20.348 --> 00:12:25.236
The program observes and records; it
doesn't route packets or pick a vendor.

190
00:12:25.236 --> 00:12:28.450
But one part applies
right now: the evidence.

191
00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:38.013
Whether or not the governed path is adopted,
every SD-WAN and SASE decision should

192
00:12:38.013 --> 00:12:43.313
land as a record: who decided, under which
policy, for which identity, and when.

193
00:12:43.313 --> 00:12:47.739
Each adapter declares what it does and
where it sits relative to the boundary,

194
00:12:47.739 --> 00:12:51.474
and one that fits nowhere needs a
decision before it's registered.

195
00:12:51.474 --> 00:12:53.886
The table shows the drift worth watching,

196
00:12:53.886 --> 00:12:57.787
like a telemetry chain that quietly
breaks at a boundary crossing.

197
00:12:57.787 --> 00:13:02.046
The book is candid about the limits:
some products don't emit records,

198
00:13:02.046 --> 00:13:05.924
so you may have to derive them from
logs, and label them that way.

199
00:13:05.924 --> 00:13:08.310
Next, what applications need.

200
00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:14.285
The obligations run both ways.

201
00:13:14.285 --> 00:13:17.470
Re-routing a branch doesn't
break TCP on its own;

202
00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:22.248
changing the source address does, so
keep each branch's egress stable.

203
00:13:22.248 --> 00:13:25.974
Give every branch a defined
path to a domain controller,

204
00:13:25.974 --> 00:13:28.404
or sign-ins quietly fall back to NTLM.

205
00:13:28.404 --> 00:13:33.265
Keep sign-in and resource traffic
leaving from the same known addresses,

206
00:13:33.265 --> 00:13:36.748
or location policies weaken
and tokens get shorter.

207
00:13:36.748 --> 00:13:40.273
Prioritize small identity
exchanges over backups.

208
00:13:40.273 --> 00:13:43.259
And watch for stalled
transfers after a path change.

209
00:13:43.259 --> 00:13:48.235
The thread is address continuity, the one
fix here a branch edge really delivers.

210
00:13:48.235 --> 00:13:50.450
That closes Module 3.

211
00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:59.100
Module 4 is the book's worked case: about
fifteen hundred field offices, and a

212
00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:04.475
proposal to spend fifty million dollars
refreshing a content server in every one.

213
00:14:04.475 --> 00:14:07.763
Every cost here is an
illustrative planning estimate.

214
00:14:07.763 --> 00:14:12.449
We'll test the request, lay out the network
alternative, walk through its sizing,

215
00:14:12.449 --> 00:14:17.130
cost and roadmap, and then look at the
interim most agencies are really in.

216
00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:21.980
Here's the case.

217
00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:26.272
The proposal works out to about
thirty-three thousand dollars an office,

218
00:14:26.272 --> 00:14:28.418
roughly twice a fully loaded server.

219
00:14:28.418 --> 00:14:31.643
So the first question is what
that number really includes.

220
00:14:31.643 --> 00:14:33.492
But the published limits settle it.

221
00:14:33.492 --> 00:14:36.847
One distribution point serves
up to four thousand clients,

222
00:14:36.847 --> 00:14:41.092
so seventy thousand endpoints need
about twenty-four, not fifteen hundred.

223
00:14:41.092 --> 00:14:43.868
The other fourteen hundred
and seventy-six exist

224
00:14:43.868 --> 00:14:46.780
only because the branch
circuits were once too slow.

225
00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:49.747
The timing is off too,
since the money lands on

226
00:14:49.747 --> 00:14:52.955
a tier the device strategy
is already shrinking.

227
00:14:52.955 --> 00:14:55.942
The role isn't obsolete;
one per office is.

228
00:14:55.942 --> 00:14:58.190
So what does the alternative look like?

229
00:15:01.940 --> 00:15:05.447
The alternative sizes content
to clients, not offices,

230
00:15:05.447 --> 00:15:08.252
and fixes transport at
the transport layer.

231
00:15:08.252 --> 00:15:13.517
Twelve hubs in existing facilities carry
SD-WAN aggregation, regional breakout,

232
00:15:13.517 --> 00:15:16.817
security inspection and
two content servers each:

233
00:15:16.817 --> 00:15:20.039
twenty-four in all, about
three quarters utilized.

234
00:15:20.039 --> 00:15:25.465
Offices get a right-sized internet circuit,
a five G backup and no local server.

235
00:15:25.465 --> 00:15:30.913
Peer caching keeps most bytes off the WAN,
with one SD-WAN detail worth catching:

236
00:15:30.913 --> 00:15:35.272
behind a hub, Delivery Optimization
needs group or subnet mode,

237
00:15:35.272 --> 00:15:37.703
or every office looks like one site.

238
00:15:37.703 --> 00:15:40.970
Next, the branch hardware and its cost.

239
00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:47.655
Offices fall into three
tiers by seat count,

240
00:15:47.655 --> 00:15:51.895
and the book uses one vendor's
line purely as a costable example.

241
00:15:51.895 --> 00:15:56.237
Year one capital, edges, hubs,
the control plane and services,

242
00:15:56.237 --> 00:15:58.807
comes to about ten million dollars.

243
00:15:58.807 --> 00:16:01.982
The design rules are where
these projects usually go wrong.

244
00:16:01.982 --> 00:16:06.532
A small office needs a symmetric,
SLA-backed circuit, not a gigabit.

245
00:16:06.532 --> 00:16:10.258
Five G is for continuity,
not feature-update waves.

246
00:16:10.258 --> 00:16:13.657
Check that two fiber providers
aren't sharing one conduit.

247
00:16:13.657 --> 00:16:16.971
And license encrypted throughput
to match the circuit,

248
00:16:16.971 --> 00:16:21.220
because that's the most expensive
costing mistake in this class of edge.

249
00:16:21.220 --> 00:16:23.370
Now, the running costs.

250
00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:28.997
On the program's assumptions,

251
00:16:28.997 --> 00:16:33.454
the new network costs about fifteen
point seven million a year to run,

252
00:16:33.454 --> 00:16:38.458
and retiring MPLS saves about twenty-one
point six, so it pays back by year four.

253
00:16:38.458 --> 00:16:40.635
Notice the security service line:

254
00:16:40.635 --> 00:16:44.757
per-user SASE licensing is the
cost most comparisons leave out.

255
00:16:44.757 --> 00:16:48.732
But the whole case turns on one
number the analysis doesn't have,

256
00:16:48.732 --> 00:16:50.795
what MPLS costs per office today.

257
00:16:50.795 --> 00:16:54.220
Breakeven is about eight hundred
seventy-three dollars a month.

258
00:16:54.220 --> 00:16:56.695
Above it, the program funds itself.

259
00:16:56.695 --> 00:16:59.358
Below it, argue capability instead.

260
00:16:59.358 --> 00:17:00.896
Validate that first.

261
00:17:00.896 --> 00:17:03.130
Then, the order of work.

262
00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:10.187
The roadmap starts with
accreditation, because that,

263
00:17:10.187 --> 00:17:12.818
not buying equipment,
is the critical path.

264
00:17:12.818 --> 00:17:16.217
Hubs come first: twelve sites
instead of fifteen hundred

265
00:17:16.217 --> 00:17:19.979
shrinks the accreditation problem
by two orders of magnitude.

266
00:17:19.979 --> 00:17:23.741
Offices then move in waves as
their MPLS contracts renew,

267
00:17:23.741 --> 00:17:28.142
and no circuit ends until its
replacement telemetry is confirmed.

268
00:17:28.142 --> 00:17:31.805
And the last content server
retires on gates, not a date.

269
00:17:31.805 --> 00:17:33.338
Apps, updates, imaging,

270
00:17:33.338 --> 00:17:37.680
caching and compliance evidence
all have to move off it first.

271
00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:40.830
Close the gates in month
thirty, retire it then.

272
00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:43.180
But what if no program is funded?

273
00:17:46.930 --> 00:17:51.700
For many agencies the target is years
away: acquisition, budget cycles,

274
00:17:51.700 --> 00:17:55.730
site logistics, and the same
staff on three other deadlines.

275
00:17:55.730 --> 00:17:57.255
That interval isn't empty.

276
00:17:57.255 --> 00:18:01.384
Four of the six things a branch edge
provides are partly available now,

277
00:18:01.384 --> 00:18:03.805
like path measurement and stable egress.

278
00:18:03.805 --> 00:18:07.485
And there's a fork coming
whether you plan for it or not:

279
00:18:07.485 --> 00:18:12.168
a directive requires end-of-support
edge devices out by August 2027.

280
00:18:12.168 --> 00:18:16.268
Replace like for like, and you've closed
the architecture question for years.

281
00:18:16.268 --> 00:18:19.460
Write the target attributes
into that purchase instead;

282
00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:22.042
it costs almost nothing
at requirements time.

283
00:18:22.042 --> 00:18:24.160
Finally, proving it paid.

284
00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:30.942
The cost structure
changes what you pay for:

285
00:18:30.942 --> 00:18:35.910
MPLS scales with sites and ports,
internet plus SASE scales with users.

286
00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:39.735
Price your own circuits rather than
trusting a marketing multiple.

287
00:18:39.735 --> 00:18:42.709
Then a warning the book
labels as its own inference:

288
00:18:42.709 --> 00:18:45.682
migrate to cloud before
you can measure transport,

289
00:18:45.682 --> 00:18:49.647
and slowness gets blamed on the cloud,
so the fixes raise cloud spend.

290
00:18:49.647 --> 00:18:51.198
The cure is measurement.

291
00:18:51.198 --> 00:18:56.922
Pair two similar branches, instrument both
for thirty days, change one, and compare.

292
00:18:56.922 --> 00:18:59.835
That's a number a budget
examiner can't take apart.

293
00:18:59.835 --> 00:19:01.710
Let's finish with what to do.

294
00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:08.248
To close: each decision has one owner.

295
00:19:08.248 --> 00:19:11.786
The CISO on the governor's
controls and leaving MTIPS,

296
00:19:11.786 --> 00:19:17.135
the authorizing official on the boundary
change and the Microsoft 365 exception,

297
00:19:17.135 --> 00:19:19.809
the system owner on each site's readiness.

298
00:19:19.809 --> 00:19:23.421
The evidence is the path
policy, failover results,

299
00:19:23.421 --> 00:19:27.297
telemetry, decision records
and honest limitations.

300
00:19:27.297 --> 00:19:32.256
And plenty starts without a contract:
write the path policy, stabilize egress,

301
00:19:32.256 --> 00:19:36.363
put the right attributes in the
refresh, validate your MPLS cost,

302
00:19:36.363 --> 00:19:39.772
start CLAW onboarding, and
measure a pair of branches.

303
00:19:39.772 --> 00:19:42.756
Physics for the pipe,
governor before traffic,

304
00:19:42.756 --> 00:19:46.447
boundary before cutover,
evidence before a circuit ends.

305
00:19:46.447 --> 00:19:47.270
Thank you.
